* Methods and pointer indirection

Comparing the previous two programs, you might notice that
functions with a pointer argument must take a pointer:

⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000001⟫Compile error!⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000002⟫while methods with pointer receivers take either a value or a pointer as the
receiver when they are called:

⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000003⟫

For the statement ⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000004⟫v.Scale(5)⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000005⟫, even though ⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000006⟫v⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000007⟫ is a value and not a pointer,
the method with the pointer receiver is called automatically.
That is, as a convenience, ⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000008⟫ interprets the statement ⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000009⟫v.Scale(5)⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000010⟫ as
⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000011⟫(&v).Scale(5)⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000012⟫ since the ⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000013⟫Scale⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000014⟫ method has a pointer receiver.

⟪GTI18N_39fb725b_000015⟫
